          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My child has a temperature of 39.5 and a rash that does not go pale when I press a glass on it. How urgent is this, and what should I do right now?",
          "answer": "**This is an emergency. Call emergency services now, or take your child to the nearest emergency department right away. Do not wait to see if it gets better.**\n\nA fever with a rash that does not fade under a glass is one of the warning signs listed for meningitis and meningococcal infection. The guidance says to \"call emergency services immediately\" for this combination (*Signs suggesting meningitis or meningococcal infection*). The same document lists this rash as a sign of sepsis, a life-threatening response to infection (*Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection*).\n\nMy urgency check returned \"routine,\" because this description did not match its fixed table. That does not change the advice. The emergency guidance above names this exact combination, so I am treating it as an emergency.\n\n**While you are getting there:**\n- Call emergency services, or ask someone else to, while you travel. Tell them the child has a fever and a rash that does not fade under a glass.\n- Keep your child lying down and as calm as you can.\n- Do not give anything to delay getting care. Do not wait to see if the rash changes.\n- If you are unsure whether to go, go.\n\n**Get help straight away if you notice any of these:**\n- Drowsiness, confusion, or difficulty waking.\n- A seizure (fit).\n- A high-pitched or moaning cry, or irritability when handled.\n- Refusing feeds.\n- A bulging soft spot on a baby's head.\n- Floppiness or stiffness.\n- Pale, blotchy, mottled, ashen, or blue skin.\n- Cold hands and feet with a fever.\n- Very fast breathing, or breathing that looks hard or noisy.\n- Not passing urine for many hours.\n- Severe pain in the muscles or joints, or a sense that your child is much more unwell than ever before.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
